          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+cosmology"} standard model of cosmology
            including
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+energy"} dark energy
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+matter"} dark matter
            is in very good agreement with observation on scales above those of galaxies, but in its standard version becomes problematic below this scale (there are variants that address this, such as
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fuzzy+dark+matter"} fuzzy dark matter
          %p
            Curiously though, the particular behaviour of experimental data on these “small” cosmological scales turns out to have a remarkably simple and universal phenomenological fit by a simple modification of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/force"} force
            law of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Newtonian+mechanics"} Newtonian mechanics
            (“Newton’s third law”, corrections from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
            are typically very small for the effects in question).
          %p
            %em MOND
            is the abbreviation for this
            %em modified Newtonian dynamics
            (
            %a{:href => "#Milgrom83a"}> Milgrom 83 a
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Milgrom83b"} Milgrom 83 b
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Milgrom83c"} Milgrom 83 c
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %mi m
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  F = m a
          %p
            relating the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/force"} force
            experienced by a body of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} mass
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
            to its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/acceleration"} acceleration
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            by an expression of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %mi m
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  F = m \,\mu(a/a_0)\, a
          %p
            for some acceleration scale
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_0
            and some interpolating function
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu \colon \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}
          %p
            The motivation is that choosing the constant
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_0
            and the function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            suitably, then such a modified formula fits the rotation-velocities of observed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/galaxies"} galaxies
            in dependence of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/radius"} radius
            remarkably well, something which the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+cosmology"} standard model of cosmology
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+matter"} dark matter
            still has some problems with (but see
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#KaplinghatTurner02"} Kaplinghat-Turner 02
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BLSF09"} BLSF 09
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Chan13"} Chan 13
          %p
            In 2020, David Merritt wrote a review of MOND and dark matter which stated that the observational evidence favors MOND over dark matter (
            %a{:href => "#Merritt20"}> Merritt20
            ).
          %h2#relativistic_extensions_of_mond Relativistic extensions of MOND
          %p
            The original MOND model by Milgrom is an ad-hoc such modification of the basic laws of physics and breaks many established properties of physics, such as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
            and various
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conservation+laws"} conservation laws
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Tensor-Vector-Scalar gravity (
                %a{:href => "#Bekenstein04"}> Bekenstein 04
                , “TeVeS”)
            %li
              %p
                Aether scalar tensor theory (
                %a{:href => "#SkordisZlosnik21"}> SkordisZlosnik21
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "#SkordisZlosnik22"} SkordisZlosnik22
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "#MMH23"} MMH23
            %li
              %p
                Bimetric MOND (
                %a{:href => "#Milgrom22"}> Milgrom22
                )
            %li
              %p
                Khronometric MOND (
                %a{:href => "#Flanagan23"}> Flanagan23
                )
          %p
            In addition, there are also studies of relativistic MOND gravity in general (
            %a{:href => "#TMZ23"}> TMZ23
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#THCZ23"} THCZ23
          %p#TheseTheories
            These theories tend to postulate new
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+field+theory"} relativistic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            adjoined to plain
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+gravity"} Einstein gravity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+field+theory"} relativistic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interactions"} interactions
            of these hypothetical new fields is designed to mimic the originally postulated MOdfied Newtonian Dynamics in appropriate regime, to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenologically
            viable they must otherwise be effectively unobservable and as such are rather like the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+matter"} dark matter
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            whose introduction the original idea of MOND it was to avoid. Therefore, the difference between the oxymoronic “relativistic MOND” and mainstream
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+matter"} dark matter
            is now more one of style and focus than of principle.
          %h3#tensorvectorscalar_gravity Tensor-Vector-Scalar gravity
          %p
            The first such proposal for such a theory apart from
            = succeed "+" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+matter"} dark matter
            itself (
            %a{:href => "#KaplinghatTurner02"}> Kaplinghat-Turner 02
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BLSF09"} BLSF 09
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Chan13"} Chan 13
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+field"} scalar field
            (
            %a{:href => "#Bekenstein04"}> Bekenstein 04
            , “TeVeS”), themselves thus otherwise unobserved “dark fields”, as it were, but, as opposed to say the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axion"} axion
            dark matter candidate, not motivated beyond the desire to fit galaxy rotation curves.
          %p
            From
            %a{:href => "#Bekenstein04"} Bekenstein 04, p. 9
            one sees explicitly that TeVeS is just Einstein-gravity coupled to peculiar “matter” fields:
          %p
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/TeVeSActionFunctional.jpg", :width => "600"}/
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              An alternative proposal is that of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fuzzy+dark+matter"} fuzzy dark matter
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+matter"} dark matter
              which is so extremely light that its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Broglie+wavelength"} de Broglie wavelength
              is of the scales of galaxies. Such
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fuzzy+dark+matter"} fuzzy dark matter
              models make the same prediction as cold dark matter on cosmological scales, but change behaviour on scales of galaxies due to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum
              nature of these light particles becoming relevant here. This change of behaviour has been argued to account for the kind of effects that MOND is a fit for.
          %h2#references References
          %h3#original_articles Original articles
          %p The concept of MOND is due to
          %ul
            %li#Milgrom83a
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mordehai+Milgrom"}> Mordehai Milgrom
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A modification of the Newtonian dynamics as a possible alternative to the hidden mass hypothesis
            %li#Milgrom83b
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mordehai+Milgrom"}> Mordehai Milgrom
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A modification of the Newtonian dynamics - Implications for galaxies
            %li#Milgrom83c
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mordehai+Milgrom"}> Mordehai Milgrom
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A modification of the Newtonian dynamics - Implications for galaxy systems
          %p There are many relativistic extensions of MOND, which are listed in the following section:
          %h3#ReferencesRelativisticModels Relativistic MOND models
          %p
            On
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} relativistic
            MOND
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28in+theoretical+physics%29"} models
          %ul
            %li#Bekenstein04
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Bekenstein"}> Jacob Bekenstein
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Relativistic gravitation theory for the modified Newtonian dynamics paradigm
                %strong 70
                8 (2004) 083509, &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0403694"}> arXiv:astro-ph/0403694
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.70.083509"} doi:10.1103/PhysRevD.70.083509
            %li#SkordisZlosnik21
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Constantinos+Skordis"}> Constantinos Skordis
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+Zlosnik"} Tom Zlosnik
                = succeed "." do
                  %em A new relativistic theory for Modified Newtonian Dynamics
                %strong 127
                16 (2021) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.127.161302"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRevLett.127.161302
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.00082"} arXiv:2007.00082
            %li#SkordisZlosnik22
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Constantinos+Skordis"}> Constantinos Skordis
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+Zlosnik"} Tom Zlosnik
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Aether scalar tensor theory: Linear stability on Minkowski space
                %strong 106
                10 (2022) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.106.104041"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRevD.106.104041
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.13287"} arXiv:2109.13287
            %li#Milgrom22
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mordehai+Milgrom"}> Mordehai Milgrom
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Broader view of bimetric MOND
                %strong 106
                8 (2022) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.106.084010"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRevD.106.084010
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.10882"} arXiv:2208.10882
            %li#MMH23
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tobias+Mistele"}> Tobias Mistele
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stacy+McGaugh"} Stacy McGaugh
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sabine+Hossenfelder"} Sabine Hossenfelder
                %em Aether Scalar Tensor theory confronted with weak lensing data at small accelerations
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.03499"}> arXiv:2301.03499
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#TMZ23
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Thomas"}> Daniel Thomas
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ali+Mozaffari"} Ali Mozaffari
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+Zlosnik"} Tom Zlosnik
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Consistent cosmological structure formation on all scales in relativistic extensions of MOND
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.00038"} arXiv:2303.00038
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1475-7516/2023/06/006"} doi:10.1088/1475-7516/2023/06/006
            %li#Flanagan23
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eanna+Flanagan"}> Eanna Flanagan
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Khronometric theories of modified Newtonian dynamics
                %strong 958
                2 (2023) 107 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.14846"}> arXiv:2302.14846
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/ad003d"} doi:10.3847/1538-4357/ad003d
            %li#THCZ23
              %p
                Shuxun Tian, Shaoqi Hou, Shuo Cao, Zong-Hong Zhu:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Time evolution of the local gravitational parameters and gravitational wave polarizations in a relativistic MOND theory
                %strong 107
                4 (2023) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.107.044062"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRevD.107.044062
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.13304"} arXiv:2302.13304
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Bojowald"}> Martin Bojowald
              , Erick I. Duque:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Emergent Modified Gravity
              %strong 41
              (2024) 095008 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.06375"}> arXiv:2404.06375
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1088/1361-6382/ad36a8"} doi:10.1088/1361-6382/ad36a8
          %h3#review Review
          %p General review includes
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Benoît Famaey and Stacy S. McGaugh,
                = succeed ",2012," do
                  %em Modified Newtonian Dynamics (MOND): Observational Phenomenology and Relativistic Extensions
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1112.3960"} arXiv:1112.3960
            %li
              %p
                Joe Silk, Gary A. Mamon,
                %em The Current Status of Galaxy Formation
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1207.3080"}> arXiv:1207.3080
                )
            %li
              %p
                Wikipedia,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Modified_Newtonian_dynamics"} Modified Newtonian dynamics
            %li
              %p
                Wikipedia,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tensor%E2%80%93vector%E2%80%93scalar_gravity"} Tensor–vector–scalar gravity
            %li#Peebles22
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Phillip+James+E.+Peebles"}> Phillip James E. Peebles
                , §5.7 “Why is MOND Successful but Unpoular” of:
                %em Anomalies in Physical Cosmology
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.05018"}> arXiv:2208.05018
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#Merritt20
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Merritt"}> David Merritt
                ,
                = succeed "." do
                  %em A Philosophical Approach to MOND: Assessing the Milgromian Research Program in Cosmology
          %h3#experimental_constraints Experimental constraints
          %p The stark failure of plain MOND to fit data on large cosmological scales is highlighted in
          %ul
            %li#Dodelson11
              %p
                Scott Dodelson,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Real Problem with MOND
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1112.1320"} arXiv:1112.1320
            %li
              %p
                Kris Pardo,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Spergel"} David Spergel
                %em What is the price of abandoning dark matter? Cosmological constraints on alternative gravity theories
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.00555"}> arXiv:2007.00555
                )
          %p The instability of its relativistic completion by TeVeS was pointed out in
          %ul
            %li
              Michael D. Seifert,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Stability of spherically symmetric solutions in modified theories of gravity
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/gr-qc/0703060"} arXiv:gr-qc/0703060
          %p#ConstraintsFromMergingNeutronStars
            The detection of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravitational+waves"} gravitational waves
            coincident with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+radiation"} electromagnetic radiation
            from merging
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neutron+stars"} neutron stars
            (event GW170817,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "LIGO-Virgo#LIGOVirgo17"} LIGO-Virgo 17
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Jose María Ezquiaga, Miguel Zumalacárregui,
                %em Dark Energy after GW170817
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.05901"}> arXiv:1710.05901
                )
            %li
              %p
                Sibel Boran, Shantanu Desai, Emre Kahya, Richard Woodard,
                %em GW170817 Falsifies Dark Matter Emulators
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.06168"}> arXiv:1710.06168
                )
          %p
            The observation of a galaxy that does not exhibit the effect which MOND claims is universal (otherwise attributed to the presence of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+matter"} dark matter
          %ul
            %li#vanDokkum18
              Pieter van Dokkum et.al.
              = succeed "," do
                %em A galaxy lacking dark matter
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.10237"} arXiv:1803.10237
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.nature.com/articles/nature25767"} doi:10.1038/nature25767
          %blockquote
            %p
              Regardless of the formation history of NGC1052–DF2, its existence has implications for the dark matter paradigm. Our results demonstrate that dark matter is separable from galaxies, which is (under certain circumstances) expected if it is bound to baryons through nothing but gravity. The “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bullet+cluster"}> bullet cluster
              ” demonstrates that dark matter does not always trace the bulk of the baryonic mass, which in clusters is in the form of gas. NGC1052–DF2 enables us to make the complementary point that dark matter does not always coincide with galaxies either: it is a distinct “substance” that may or may not be present in a galaxy. Furthermore, and paradoxically, the existence of NGC1052–DF2 may falsify alternatives to dark matter. In theories such as
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MOND"} MOND
              and the recently proposed emergent gravity paradigm a “dark matter” signature should always be detected, as it is an unavoidable consequence of the presence of ordinary matter. In fact, it had been argued previously that the apparent absence of galaxies such as NGC1052–DF2 constituted a falsification of the standard cosmological model, and evidence for modified gravity.
          %p The observation that wide binaries do not seem to have MOND effects:
          %ul
            %li
              %span.newWikiWord>
                Charalambos Pittordis
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Charalambos+Pittordis"} ?
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  Will Sutherland
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Will+Sutherland"} ?
              = succeed "." do
                %em Wide Binaries from GAIA EDR3: preference for GR over MOND?
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.21105/astro.2205.02846"} doi:10.21105/astro.2205.02846
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.02846"} arXiv:2205.02846
          %p The observation that SPARC galaxies prefer dark matter over MOND:
          %ul
            %li
              %span.newWikiWord>
                Mariia Khelashvili
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Mariia+Khelashvili"} ?
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  Anton Rudakovskyi
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Anton+Rudakovskyi"} ?
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sabine+Hossenfelder"} Sabine Hossenfelder
              %em SPARC galaxies prefer Dark Matter over MOND
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.10202"}> arXiv:2401.10202
              )
          %h3#mond_phenomenology_from_actual_dark_matter MOND phenomenology from actual dark matter
          %p
            Derivations of the MOND phenomenology from actual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+matter"} dark matter
            is discussed in the following articles:
          %ul
            %li#KaplinghatTurner02
              %p
                Manoj Kaplinghat, Michael S. Turner,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em How Cold Dark Matter Theory Explains Milgrom’s Law
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0107284"} arXiv:astro-ph/0107284
            %li#BLSF09
              %p
                Jean-Philippe Bruneton, Stefano Liberati, Lorenzo Sindoni, Benoit Famaey,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Reconciling MOND and dark matter?
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0811.3143"} arXiv:0811.3143
            %li#Chan13
              %p
                Man Ho Chan,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Reconciliation of MOND and Dark Matter theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.6801"} arXiv:1310.6801
          %p
            and specifically for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axion"} axionic
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fuzzy+dark+matter"} fuzzy dark matter
          %ul
            %li#HOTW16
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lam+Hui"}> Lam Hui
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jeremiah+Ostriker"} Jeremiah Ostriker
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Scott+Tremaine"} Scott Tremaine
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"} Edward Witten
              = succeed "," do
                %em On the hypothesis that cosmological dark matter is composed of ultra-light bosons
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.08297"} arXiv:1610.08297
          %p
            and for fuzzy dark matter with
            = succeed "-effects" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superfluid"} superfluid
          %ul
            %li#BerezhianiKhoury15
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lasha+Berezhiani"}> Lasha Berezhiani
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Justin+Khoury"} Justin Khoury
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Theory of Dark Matter Superfluidity
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1507.01019"} arXiv:1507.01019
            %li#Khoury16
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Justin+Khoury"}> Justin Khoury
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Another Path for the Emergence of Modified Galactic Dynamics from Dark Matter Superfluidity
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.05961"} arXiv:1602.05961
          %h3#hybrid_mond_dark_matter_models Hybrid MOND dark matter models
          %p
            Hybrid models which contain both
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MOND"} MOND
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+matter"} dark matter
            are explored in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tobias+Mistele"}> Tobias Mistele
                ,
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Cherenkov radiation from stars constrains hybrid MOND dark matter models
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1088/1475-7516/2022/11/008"} doi:10.1088/1475-7516/2022/11/008
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.16954"} arXiv:2103.16954
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tobias+Mistele"}> Tobias Mistele
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A novel Cherenkov radiation constraint for hybrid MOND dark matter models
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.14308"} arXiv:2208.14308
          %h3#other_references Other references
          %p For possible MOND effects in the Solar System:
          %ul
            %li
              Cezary Migaszewski,
              %em On the origin of extreme trans-Neptunian objects within Modified Newtonian Dynamics
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.13339"}> arXiv:2303.13339
              )
          %p MOND as an alternative to Planet Nine hypothesis:
          %ul
            %li
              Katherine Jones-Smith, Harsh Mathur,
              %em Modified Newtonian Dynamics as an Alternative to the Planet Nine Hypothesis
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.00576"}> arXiv:2304.00576
              )
        .revisedby
          %p
